<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How much paint common rooms need</h2>
    <p>The table below assumes 8 ft ceilings, 1 door, 2 windows, 2 coats, and 350 sq ft per gallon. It covers the walls only. The last column is what to actually buy, because a 3rd gallon is often more paint than the job needs.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Room</th><th class="num">Wall area</th><th class="num">Paint for 2 coats</th><th>What to buy</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>10 × 10 ft bedroom</td><td class="num">269 sq ft</td><td class="num">1.54 gal</td><td>2 gallons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 × 12 ft bedroom</td><td class="num">301 sq ft</td><td class="num">1.72 gal</td><td>2 gallons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 × 12 ft room</td><td class="num">333 sq ft</td><td class="num">1.90 gal</td><td>2 gallons, with little to spare</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 × 14 ft room</td><td class="num">365 sq ft</td><td class="num">2.09 gal</td><td>2 gallons plus 1 quart</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 × 14 ft room, 9 ft ceiling</td><td class="num">417 sq ft</td><td class="num">2.38 gal</td><td>2 gallons plus 2 quarts, or 3 gallons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>14 × 16 ft living room</td><td class="num">429 sq ft</td><td class="num">2.45 gal</td><td>3 gallons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16 × 20 ft great room</td><td class="num">525 sq ft</td><td class="num">3.00 gal</td><td>3 gallons plus 1 quart</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>2 rows deserve a second look. The 12 × 14 room needs only 0.09 gallon more than 2 gallons, about a third of a quart, so a single quart covers the gap for far less than a 3rd gallon. The 16 × 20 room needs exactly 3.00 gallons on paper, which leaves nothing for touch-ups, a thirsty wall, or the paint left in the roller tray. Add a quart.</p>
    <p>A quart usually costs well over a quarter of a gallon's price. Once you need more than 2 quarts, the extra gallon is normally the better buy.</p>

    <h2>Ceilings are a separate job</h2>
    <p>A ceiling's area is just length × width, so the 12 × 14 room above has a 168 sq ft ceiling. That takes 0.48 gallon for 1 coat or 0.96 gallon for 2. One gallon of flat ceiling paint covers it. Ceiling paint is usually a different product from wall paint, so tick "Include ceiling" only when both surfaces get the same paint.</p>

    <h2>What changes real coverage</h2>
    <p>The 350 sq ft figure assumes smooth, sealed drywall and a good roller. Most interior paint cans list somewhere between 250 and 400 sq ft per gallon. Lower the coverage field when any of these apply:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>New or patched drywall.</strong> Bare joint compound soaks up paint. Prime first; primer has its own coverage rate on its can.</li>
      <li><strong>Texture.</strong> Knockdown walls, popcorn ceilings, brick, and block have more surface than their flat dimensions suggest. Try 250 sq ft per gallon.</li>
      <li><strong>Big color changes.</strong> Going from dark red to off-white often needs a 3rd coat or a tinted primer. Set coats to 3 rather than hoping.</li>
      <li><strong>Higher ceilings.</strong> Wall area grows in step with height. Going from 8 ft to 9 ft adds 12.5% more wall, which is why the 9 ft room in the table needs 0.29 gallon more.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Mistakes that leave you a gallon short</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Forgetting the 2nd coat.</strong> A single coat rarely hides the old color evenly. The calculator multiplies by coats for this reason.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipping closets.</strong> A reach-in closet adds 60 to 100 sq ft of wall. Add its dimensions as a 2nd room or add its area in "I know the sq ft" mode.</li>
      <li><strong>Buying cans from different batches.</strong> Tinted paint can vary slightly from can to can. Buy everything at once, then pour all cans into one bucket and mix ("boxing") before you start.</li>
      <li><strong>Counting trim in the walls.</strong> Doors, baseboards, and window casings usually get semi-gloss or enamel. Estimate them separately.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Paint questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many gallons of paint do I need for a 12 × 12 room?</summary><p>About 1.9 gallons for 2 coats on the walls, assuming 8 ft ceilings, 1 door, 2 windows, and 350 sq ft per gallon. Buy 2 gallons. Add a quart if the walls are textured or the color change is dramatic.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does primer count as a coat?</summary><p>No. Primer seals the surface and evens out color, but it is not the finish. Estimate primer separately using the coverage printed on its can, then plan 2 finish coats on top.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is it cheaper to buy quarts or another gallon?</summary><p>For 1 or 2 quarts of shortfall, quarts usually cost less. Past 2 quarts, a gallon is usually the better value, and the leftover is useful for touch-ups.</p></details>
  </section>
